WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.230
INTRO:
Jailbreaking was one of the biggest parts

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.760
of my childhood.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.340
Whenever Apple released a new version of iOS,
the entire jailbreaking community would come

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.340
alive.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.650
We would see dozens of videos covering the
latest exploits and kernels until we finally

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:19.510
got the latest jailbreaking tool from OGs
like redsn0w, limera1n, Absinthe, TaiG, and

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:20.510
Pangu.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:25.359
Sometimes, these tools were only capable of
achieving a tethered jailbreak which meant

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:29.550
that you had to plug your phone into a computer
if you wanted to reboot your device.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:34.340
I remember always avoiding the tethered versions
and impatiently waiting for an untethered

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.340
version.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.199
In fact, I used to stay on the last major
version of iOS until an untethered jailbreak

00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:41.280
was available for the new version.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.489
All of this was just so that us Apple fanboys
could have a few cool tweaks that made our

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:49.789
devices unique whether that be getting dark
mode, customizing the carrier text, setting

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:53.489
up widgets, creating video wallpapers, and
so on and so forth.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:57.170
And the platform that made it all possible
was Cydia.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:02.249
If you’re not familiar with Cydia, it was
basically the app store for jailbroken devices.

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:06.130
This is where you would add new sources and
download the latest tweaks.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:10.060
And whenever you opened Cydia, one of the
first things you would see is that it was

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:14.030
created by a man named Jay Freeman also known
as Saurik.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.610
I always wondered who this guy was as a kid,
but ever since I stopped jailbreaking, I completely

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.490
forgot about him until today.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:27.320
So, here’s the story of Cydia’s founder
and what he’s up to today.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:33.479
JAY FREEMAN:
Taking a look back, the story of Jay Freeman

00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:37.220
dates back to November 27, 1981 to Chicago,
Illinois.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:42.100
We don’t really know much about his early
childhood, but I think it’s safe to assume

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.400
that he was pretty involved coding and tech
given that he was publishing papers and launching

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.299
software from very early on.

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:53.740
For example, in 2002, Jay built a decompiler
called Anakrino for the .NET framework.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:59.030
If you’re not familiar with decompiling,
it’s basically reverse engineering software.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:02.719
Whenever you write a piece of code and run
it, the computer will compile the code and

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:06.799
convert it into something that it can understand
called machine level language.

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:10.550
Oftentimes, this means converting everything
to 1s and 0s.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:14.739
Building a compiler itself is quite complex
given that you have to account for so many

00:02:14.739 --> 00:02:17.349
different factors such as errors in the code.

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:22.450
However, this is not what Jay was doing, he
was decompiling software meaning that he was

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.269
trying to convert 0s and 1s back into english
code.

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:28.920
Now, I can’t understate how difficult this
is.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.520
Whenever you compile code, computers don’t
just directly convert it into 1s and 0s.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.390
Rather, they play around with the code to
increase efficiency, reduce redundancy and

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.390
so on.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:43.600
So, trying to piece back the original code
is usually a nightmare, yet Jay was voluntarily

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.110
developing such software when he was just
21 years old.

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:51.459
Looking back though, it makes perfect sense
that Jay was interested in such a project.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:56.069
Anyway, around the same, Jay would attend
the University of California at Santa Barbara

00:02:56.069 --> 00:02:58.150
where he studied computer science.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.720
It seems that even though Jay was a massive
fan of learning, he wasn’t the big fan of

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:03.750
traditional education.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:07.310
In fact, his high school grades weren’t
that great.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.830
However, all of this changed when he reached
college.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:14.860
You see, his school allowed students to propose
and teach classes under the guidance of professors

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.530
which quickly became one of Jay’s favorite
activities.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:22.319
In fact, Jay liked this so much that he would
actually end up spending 10 years at UC Santa

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:23.610
Barbara.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:27.430
After he completed his bachelors in 2003,
he would turn around and become a doctoral

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.319
student for the next 5 years.

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:33.770
I don’t think Jay’s primary focus was
getting the degree itself.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:37.709
After all, he never got the degree despite
pursuing it for 5 years.

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:42.140
Rather, I think what Jay really wanted was
to interact with knowledgeable peers and conduct

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.480
research which is exactly what he did.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:50.010
In 2005, for example, he coauthored a research
paper called “Java Runtime Event Specification

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.360
and Monitoring Library”.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.819
College wasn’t the only thing he did either.

00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:57.590
Throughout his entire college career, Jay
held down various tech jobs as a software

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:01.530
engineer, a software consultant, and a chief
technology officer.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.709
Considering this extensive coding background,
I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:09.110
that Jay learnt iOS development as soon as
the iPhone was released, and that brings us

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.310
to the creation of Cydia.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:16.700
FOUNDING CYDIA:
One of the first applications of Cydia can

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.950
be tracked back to an iPhone video recorder
app called Cycorder.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:24.750
Nowadays, a video recorder app might sound
trivial given that video recording has been

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:28.020
a standard feature of smartphones as long
as we can remember.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:33.160
But, there was a time when iPhone’s could
not record videos, they could only take pictures.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:38.450
In fact, it wasn’t ill iOS 3.0 that Apple
launched the video recording feature and it

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:41.610
was only available to people who owned to
latest iPhone.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:45.290
Older iPhones didn’t support video recording
even though their hardware was technically

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:46.740
capable of it.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:51.350
So, Jay decided to take this issue into his
own hands and create Cycorder.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:56.090
And it seems like Jay was able to get the
app working just fine even on the older iPhones.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:00.570
But, when he tried to post the app onto the
appstore, Apple rejected it.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:04.890
In such a situation, the average developer
would probably just be upset with Apple and

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:08.750
complain to their peers, but Jay had a far
different idea.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:12.170
Since he couldn’t post the app onto Apple’s
app store, he decided to launch it on his

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:16.410
own app store that could install apps onto
iPhones without Apple’s permission.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.500
At the time, Apple’s software wasn’t the
most secure and jailbreaking was likely not

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.270
something that Apple even gave much weight
to.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:27.250
So, it wasn’t that hard for Jay to use his
coding skills to leverage root access and

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:28.250
create Cydia.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:32.590
Fun fact, Cydia was launched even before Apple’s
app store in February of 2008.

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:37.300
And given how limiting Apple’s software
was at the time, and given that everyone who

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:41.720
had an iPhone were already tech enthusiasts,
it didn’t take long Cydia to go viral within

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:42.910
the iPhone community.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:48.810
In fact, by August of 2009, Cydia boasted
4 million users, and considering that only

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:53.560
40 million iPhones and iPods had been sold
at the time, 1 in 10 owners had jailbroken

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:54.560
their device.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.250
For obvious reasons, Jay would drop out of
his doctoral program around this same time.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.460
All of these users weren’t just for show
either.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:05.690
While Cydia was an open source project, Jay
ensured that he and and his peer developers

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:07.330
would all be paid.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:11.260
Many of his apps like Cycorder displayed ads
which allowed him to earn some money.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:15.030
Similarly, paid apps and tweaks could also
be listed on Cydia.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:19.520
Now, a lot of people would just use third
party repositories to basically pirate these

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:20.520
tweaks.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.340
But, fortunately, many tech enthusiasts wanted
to support these developers and they would

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:25.830
pay for these tweaks.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.940
By April of 2011, Cydia was pulling in $10
million in revenue per year.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:35.380
Unfortunately, when all of the operating expenses
were taken out and everyone was paid, Jay

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:40.810
was only left with roughly $250,000 per year
which is only a 2.5% net margin.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:45.400
Fortunately though, Cydia would only continue
to gain popularity as iPhone sales exploded

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:46.630
in the early 2010s.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:52.980
The problem though was that as Cydia gained
popularity, Apple became more and more annoyed.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:59.110
APPLE STRIKES BACK:
One of Apple’s first responses to any jailbreak

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.780
was to patch it as soon as possible.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:06.050
After all, to gain root access into an Apple
device, you had to leverage some sort of security

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.050
flaw.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:10.950
So, when you really think about it, jailbreakers
were actually providing free security consulting

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.740
services to Apple.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:18.810
Anyway, as Apple patched one exploit after
another, it became harder and harder to jailbreak.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:22.360
Even though more developers were working on
a jailbreak, it took longer to actually come

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.110
up with a reliable version.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:27.140
We also started seeing more restrictions when
it came to jailbreaking.

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:31.240
For example, certain jailbreaks would only
work on certain devices, and untethered jailbreaks

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:32.770
became less common.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:36.940
Aside from fighting the jailbreaking community
head on, Apple also tried to dissuade customers

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:41.220
from jailbreaking their devices by claiming
that jailbreaking was illegal which is not

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.220
exactly true.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:47.330
You see, the act of jailbreaking itself is
completely legal at least in the US thanks

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:50.710
to section 1201 of the Digital Millennium
Copyright Act.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:54.080
However, what you do with the jailbreak may
be illegal.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:59.150
For example, if you’re pirating apps, movies,
or music, then you’re clearly breaking copyright

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:00.150
laws.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.590
But, if you’re simply downloading free tweaks
from Cydia’s app store, you’re not breaking

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:05.410
any laws in the US.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:09.400
Aside from the illegality argument, Apple
also made it clear the jailbreaking may lead

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:13.160
to significantly more crashes and a much less
stable operating system.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.220
Now this is completely true.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:19.430
I think everyone who has jailbroken in the
past is very familiar with the infinite reboot

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:20.430
loop.

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:24.160
Not to mention, all of the random crashes
that occur when you’re just using the device

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:25.160
normally.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.970
Apple’s warnings weren’t that effective
though.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:32.419
Most people that were jailbreaking were well
aware of all the risks, and Apple’s warnings

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:33.969
weren’t gonna stop them.

00:08:33.969 --> 00:08:38.250
Something that would prove extremely effective
though was making jailbreaking obsolete.

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:42.089
Instead of trying to dissuade people from
jailbreaking, Apple eventually just decided

00:08:42.089 --> 00:08:45.070
to give people what they wanted without having
to jailbreak.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:49.279
For example, they introduced dark mode, they
redesigned notifications, they finally gave

00:08:49.279 --> 00:08:53.590
us widgets, they created a low power mode,
they enabled biometric authentication, and

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:54.930
so on and so forth.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:59.699
Honestly, I don’t know why they didn’t
just do this from day 1, but they eventually

00:08:59.699 --> 00:09:00.870
got there.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:05.579
And as Apple natively implemented one jailbreak
feature after another, the motivation to jailbreak

00:09:05.579 --> 00:09:06.990
quickly dissipated.

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:11.450
Jay tried to hold onto Cydia for as long as
possible, but as Cydia became a money losing

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.379
business, Jay decided to call it with Cydia
in December of 2018.

00:09:14.379 --> 00:09:18.370
He announced that Cydia would be shutting
down their purchasing mechanism which made

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:21.839
it signficnatly harder for developers to make
money from their tweaks.

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:25.509
And consequently, the entire jailbreaking
community moved over to a new jailbroken app

00:09:25.509 --> 00:09:27.199
store called Sileo.

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:30.269
And that marked the end of Cydia.

00:09:30.269 --> 00:09:35.459
JAY FREEMAN TODAY:
In terms of what Jay is doing today, he did

00:09:35.459 --> 00:09:40.019
try to sue Apple for creating a monopoly with
their app store, but as you would guess, this

00:09:40.019 --> 00:09:41.490
hasn’t really gone anywhere.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:46.089
So, Jay decided to return to something that’s
he’s familiar with which is UC Santa Barbara.

00:09:46.089 --> 00:09:50.300
In January of 2016, Jay announced that he’s
running for the Santa Barbara county board

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.710
of supervisors.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.649
Unfortunately, he lost this election.

00:09:53.649 --> 00:09:57.850
However, he would turn around and run for
the Board of Directors at the Isla Vista Community

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:02.850
Services District, and he would win and he’s
been serving in this position eversince.

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:06.670
In terms of Jay’s views on jailbreaking,
he still believes in the fundamental principles

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.110
behind jailbreaking.

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:12.149
For example, he believes that all software
should be built with screws so that they can

00:10:12.149 --> 00:10:13.420
be disassembled.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:17.350
And the jailbreaking community has made insane
progress in this realm.

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:22.269
In fact, a jailbreak called checkm8 can apparently
permanently jailbreak a subset of Apple devices

00:10:22.269 --> 00:10:24.620
and there’s nothing Apple can do about it.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:28.769
But, despite this progress, there’s no question
that jailbreaking as a whole has been on a

00:10:28.769 --> 00:10:30.440
downtrend for years now.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:33.860
I mean, just take a look at this google trends
graph.

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:37.939
Interest in jailbreaking has literally fallen
20 fold since 2013.

00:10:37.939 --> 00:10:42.490
And at the end of the day, it just comes down
to one question: What do you get in the end

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:44.040
by jailbreaking?

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:47.970
Jay Freeman says “It used to be that you
got killer features that almost were the reason

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:49.420
you owned the phone.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:52.149
And now you get a small minor modification.”

00:10:52.149 --> 00:10:57.139
And with time, this is likely only going to
get worse which will eventually make jailbreaking

00:10:57.139 --> 00:10:58.790
completely obsolete.

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:02.499
With that being said though, jailbreaking
will always remain a fond memory of the early

00:11:02.499 --> 00:11:07.120
days of the iPhone and Jay Freeman will always
be the godfather of jailbreaking.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.880
Did you guys jailbreak your Apple devices?

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:11.220
Comment that down below.

